+ self.__ttl\n        link.next = root = self.__root\n        link.prev = prev = root.prev\n        prev.next = root.prev = link\n```\n\n4. **expire_reports_removed_pairs**: `expire()` docstring says \"Remove expired items from the cache and return an iterable of the expired `(key, value)` pairs.\" And it appends `(curr.key, cache_getitem(self, curr.key))` to expired and returns `expired`. Answer: True.\n\nQuote:\n```\n    def expire(self, time=None):\n        \"\"\"Remove expired items from the cache and return an iterable of the\n        expired `(key, value)` pairs.\n\n        \"\"\"\n```\nand\n```\n            expired.append((curr.key, cache_getitem(self, curr.key)))\n```\nand `return expired`.\n\nThe task mentions \"a monotonic timer\" — TTLCache default timer is `time.monotonic`, confirmed in `__init__`: `def __init__(self, maxsize, ttl, timer=time.monotonic, getsizeof=None):`.\n\nI could also cite the `_Timer` class to confirm monotonic. Let me check the _TimedCache section before TTLCache for the _Timer class. It's around offset 11000-12000. Let me read it to get the timer context manager details.",
